---
/**
 * How a reader gets the compiler.
 *
 * TWO INSTALLERS SINCE 0.14.0. `install.sh` fetches a tarball for `uname`;
 * `install.ps1` fetches the zip that release was the first to publish. They
 * are not one command with a flag, so the page carries both and puts the
 * reader's own first: `src/components/Platform.astro` is the switch and
 * `src/lib/platform.ts` is what it decides on.
 *
 * Every command on this page is quoted out of README.md by
 * scripts/samples.mjs, and every file name and URL is built from what the two
 * installers themselves ask a release for, through
 * scripts/install-targets.mjs. A machine's name is README.md's own word for
 * it. Nothing here is typed.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Measure from "../components/Measure.astro";
import Platform from "../components/Platform.astro";
import Quote from "../components/Quote.astro";
import { latestRelease, releaseAssets, releaseUrl, windowsAsset } from "../lib/release";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const release = latestRelease();
const assets = releaseAssets(release);
const windows = windowsAsset(release);

/* The two tab labels, and the sentence in the summary, out of the same
 * machine names the download links carry. */
const posixLabel = assets.map((asset) => asset.machine).join(" / ");
const tarballs = assets.map((asset) => asset.target).join(" or ");
---

<Page
  title="Install iyi"
  summary={`Install iyi with one command: the latest release on ${posixLabel} or ${windows.machine}, then run hello. Or unpack the archive by hand, or build from source.`}
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="label">Install, <a href={releaseUrl(release)}>version {release.version}</a></p>
    <h1>One command, then hello.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two installers, one per family: a POSIX shell script over{" "}
      <code>curl</code> and <code>tar</code>, and a PowerShell one over{" "}
      <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code> and .NET's own zip reader. Each picks the
      release for your machine, checks it against the checksums the release
      publishes before it unpacks anything, and leaves a compiler with nothing
      to configure. The second line runs the first sample it shipped with.
    </p>
    <Platform
      id="install-oneliner"
      label="The machine you are installing on"
      posix={posixLabel}
      windows={windows.machine}
    >
      <Quote slot="posix" id="install" as="shell" caption="install, then run hello" copy />
      <Quote
        slot="windows"
        id="install_windows"
        as="shell"
        caption="install on Windows, then run hello"
        copy
      />
    </Platform>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h2>What it does</h2>
        <ul class="points">
          <li>
            <strong>Picks the archive for your machine:</strong> the tarball
            for <code>uname</code>, <code>{tarballs}</code>, or the{" "}
            <code>{windows.target}</code> zip on Windows. Anything else is
            refused by name, with a pointer to building from source.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Follows the latest release</strong> on GitHub, without the
            API; today that is {release.version}, from {release.date}.{" "}
            <code>IYI_VERSION={release.version}</code> pins one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Checks what it downloaded</strong> against the release's
            own checksums, and unpacks nothing when they disagree. The{" "}
            <a href={`${base}download/`}>download page</a> quotes both
            installers doing it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Unpacks into <code>~/.local</code></strong>, or into{" "}
            <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\iyi</code> on Windows, where it also
            adds <code>bin</code> to your own <code>PATH</code> and needs no
            administrator. <code>IYI_PREFIX</code> moves either one; both
            archives are relocatable.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What you get</h2>
        <ul class="points">
          <li>
            <strong>The compiler</strong>, and the same binary under Crystal's
            name for <code>.cr</code> files.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Both libraries:</strong> iyi's own,{" "}
            <Measure of="prelude_kb" unit="KB" /> on disk, and Crystal's
            standard library for <code>iyi build --crystal</code>, so your
            shards work from the first minute.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The samples</strong> under <code>share/iyi/samples</code>,
            the programs the <a href={`${base}learn/`}>lessons</a> are built on.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The tools:</strong> <code>iyi check</code>, <code>fix</code>,{" "}
            <code>test</code>, <code>doc</code>, <code>mod</code>,{" "}
            <code>lsp</code> and <code>mcp</code>, all one binary.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing to configure</strong> — <code>bin/iyi</code> finds
            its libraries beside itself, and LLVM is inside the binary. What
            no archive brings is a C toolchain: a program is linked by{" "}
            <code>cc</code>, and on Windows by <code>cl.exe</code>, so that
            machine needs the Visual C++ build tools. The compiler finds them
            and the Windows SDK through the registry, so no developer prompt
            and nothing on <code>PATH</code> is needed — and the installer
            says so when the toolset is missing.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>By hand</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The installer does two things you can do yourself. Download the archive
      for your machine, from the release this page is about, and unpack it:
    </p>
    <Platform
      id="install-by-hand"
      label="The machine you are unpacking on"
      posix={posixLabel}
      windows={windows.machine}
    >
      <div slot="posix">
        <ul class="assets">
          {assets.map((asset) => (
            <li>
              <span class="machine">{asset.machine}</span>
              <a class="url" href={asset.url}><code>{asset.url}</code></a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <Quote id="install_manual" as="shell" caption="the same two lines, by hand" copy />
      </div>
      <div slot="windows">
        <ul class="assets">
          <li>
            <span class="machine">{windows.machine}</span>
            <a class="url" href={windows.url}><code>{windows.url}</code></a>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <Quote
          id="install_manual_windows"
          as="shell"
          caption="the same two lines, by hand"
          copy
        />
      </div>
    </Platform>
    <p class="sub">
      Older versions, and the notes for each, are under{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi/releases">releases</a>. The
      checksums are published beside them, which is what the installers read.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>From source</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Other platforms, or the current tree rather than a release: clone the
      repository and <code>make</code>. It needs a Crystal compiler to
      bootstrap from and LLVM; the README's{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi#getting-it">Getting it</a>{" "}
      section names the versions and every command.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap next">
    <h2>Then</h2>
    <div class="hero-cta">
      <a class="cta" href={`${base}playground/`}>Take the tour</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}learn/`}>Learn the rule</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}#crystal`}>Coming from Crystal</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    section {
      padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
    }

    .head {
      padding-block-start: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    }

    .head h1 {
      font-size: var(--step-4);
      margin-block: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
    }

    .lede {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 60ch;
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      margin-block-end: 1.4rem;
    }

    .head :global(.console) {
      max-width: 60rem;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-2);
      margin-block-end: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sub {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
      margin-block-end: 1rem;
    }

    /* One row per tarball: the machine, then the whole URL, because a path you
     * have to assemble from a version number is a path you can get wrong. */
    .assets {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      max-width: 60rem;
    }

    .assets li {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.25rem 0.9rem;
      padding-block-end: 0.5rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .assets li:last-child {
      border-block-end: 0;
      padding-block-end: 0;
    }

    .machine {
      color: var(--ink);
      font-weight: 600;
      min-width: 9rem;
    }

    .assets .url code {
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      word-break: break-all;
    }

    .cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
      gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    }

    .points {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.8rem;
    }

    .points li {
      position: relative;
      padding-inline-start: 1.6rem;
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .points li::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      left: 0.1rem;
      top: 0.55em;
      width: 0.5rem;
      height: 0.5rem;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: var(--brand);
    }

    .points strong {
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .next {
      padding-block-end: 3rem;
    }

    .hero-cta {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
  </style>
</Page>
